    [33m([39mvalue) => (value.length < n ? message ?? [31m`Must be at least [39m${n} characters` : [34mnull[39m);

  [34mexport[39m [34mconst[39m [33mmatches[39m = (otherField, message) =>
    [33m([39mvalue, allValues) => (value === allValues[otherField] ? [34mnull[39m : message ?? [31m"Fields do not match"[39m);

  Starting set: [38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m, [38;5;153memail[39m, [38;5;153mmatches[39m. That's the floor for "works across the app"
  without inventing rules nobody asked for — [38;5;153memail[39m and [38;5;153mmatches[39m earn their place because signup and password-reset forms
  are the two most likely next consumers, and both need them. Anything beyond this set waits for a real form to ask.

  [1m[38;5;153msrc/validation/validate.js[0m — the pure core, no DOM anywhere:

  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mvalidate[39m(values, schema) [32m// → { valid: boolean, errors: { [field]: string } }[39m

  Runs each field's rules in order and [1mstops at that field's first failure[0m, so a user sees one message per field rather
  than a stack. Fields present in [38;5;153mvalues[39m but absent from the schema are ignored; fields in the schema but missing from
  [38;5;153mvalues[39m are treated as an empty string, so a schema can't be silently defeated by a missing input.

  [1m[38;5;153msrc/validation/bind-form.js[0m — the DOM layer:

  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mbindForm[39m(formEl, schema, onValid) [32m// → returns an unbind() function[39m

  Attaches a [38;5;153msubmit[39m listener, calls [38;5;153mpreventDefault()[39m, harvests values, calls [38;5;153mvalidate[39m, renders errors, and invokes
  [38;5;153monValid(values)[39m only when clean. Returning [38;5;153munbind()[39m is a small thing that costs one line now and saves you when a form
  is ever removed or re-rendered.

  The dependency direction is one-way: [38;5;153mbind-form[39m imports [38;5;153mvalidate[39m, [38;5;153mvalidate[39m imports nothing, [38;5;153mrules[39m imports nothing. The
  pure core never learns about the browser.

  On the existing [38;5;153msrc/[39m folder: [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are unrelated CommonJS Node scratch with no connection to
  the web page. I'm leaving them alone — converting them isn't part of this task. That does mean the repo will briefly
  hold both module styles; I'll note it in the spec rather than expand scope to fix it.

  Does this shape look right before I go on to the markup conventions?

